---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="Recording sessions"
   summary="a11ied can record video of real VoiceOver and NVDA sessions, creating a visual evidence trail alongside the transcript."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>Supported targets</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>Real VoiceOver sessions on macOS</li>
         <li>Real NVDA sessions on Windows</li>
         <li>Any command that starts or owns a real assistive technology session</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
         The virtual screen reader target does not support recording — it runs headlessly and
         produces transcript data instead.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Examples</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --target voiceover --recording ./recordings/voiceover-home.mov --json
a1 sr read --session &lt;sessionId&gt; --json
a1 sr stop --session &lt;sessionId&gt; --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Setup with doctor</h2>
      <p>
         The <code>doctor</code> command checks recording support and also reports how a11ied will
         handle browser-backed automation. It looks for an existing local Chromium-family browser
         first, then falls back to Playwright Chromium if it is already installed.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor
a1 doctor --json
npx playwright install chromium</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>
         Run <code>doctor</code> first if recording fails. On macOS, a11ied records video without
         microphone input. If recording works from Terminal but fails from your editor or agent,
         check that the host app has Screen Recording permission in System Settings. If a browser-backed
         command fails before it gets to recording, install Chromium with
         <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Privacy notes</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>Recordings may capture page content, system chrome, and assistive technology output.</li>
         <li>A recording documents one specific test run — include the artifact path in reports for traceability.</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>Recordings as evidence</h2>
      <p>
         Video recordings supplement the transcript. They are useful when you need to review
         exactly what the screen reader did during a real assistive technology session.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
